        "Three generic I/O scheduling classes are defined: `IOPRIO_CLASS_RT` (Realtime), `IOPRIO_CLASS_BE` (Best-Effort), and `IOPRIO_CLASS_IDLE` (Idle).",
        "`IOPRIO_CLASS_RT` (Realtime) is the highest priority, granting first access to the disk. It has 8 levels of class data and must be used carefully to avoid system starvation.",
        "`IOPRIO_CLASS_BE` (Best-Effort) is the default class, with its class data determining I/O bandwidth. It maps directly, though coarsely, to CPU nice levels (0 is highest, 7 is lowest prio). The mapping is `io_nice = (cpu_nice + 20) / 5`.",
